{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Second gate run: BLOCK on one finding, and it is the sharpest one yet\n\nVerdict BLOCK, **1 blocking finding at validity 1.0**, down from 3. The three earlier findings are\nresolved. This one is better than the ones it replaced:\n\n> AC5's committed mechanism ('a reserved tail budget that no earlier section can spend') is named\n> but its ENFORCEMENT in a non-preemptive, sequential, single-process shell script is unspecified.\n> In straight-line bash, if an earlier timeout-wrapped section blocks for its full ceiling, the\n> tail section never starts — **no budget 'reservation' can preempt an already-running blocked\n> call.**\n\nThat is correct, and it is a real gap in the plan rather than in the code. \"A reserved tail budget\"\ndescribes a *guarantee*, and in a single-process shell script there is no mechanism that could\ndeliver it as stated: once `timeout 120 du` is blocked, nothing reclaims that budget for a later\nsection. A reader given only the plan would be entitled to conclude the mechanism is impossible —\nand on the plan's own wording they would be right.\n\nThe implementation was never doing the impossible thing. It does the **inverse**, which is\nenforceable: rather than reserving budget for the tail, it *shrinks every call's ceiling as the\ndeadline approaches*, so no call can run into the reserved window in the first place. The gate's\nown suggested fix describes it almost exactly:\n\n> at start-of-run the probe computes an absolute deadline = start + (TimeoutStartSec -\n> TAIL_RESERVE); every section, before beginning, compares `now` to (deadline) and if it cannot\n> finish within remaining budget it skips…\n\nwhich is `PROBE_STARTED_AT` + `probe_budget_left` + `clamped` in change 2684. So this is a **plan\ndefect, not an implementation defect** — the description asserted an outcome and omitted the\nmechanism that makes it achievable, and the gate refused to certify a guarantee it could not see a\npath to. That is the correct call.\n\n**AC5 rewritten** to state the enforcement path concretely: the absolute deadline, the\n`min(own ceiling, budget_left)` grant, the derivation that a call starting at any t cannot end\nafter `deadline - reserve` and no sequence of them can either, and the 1s floor with its reason\n(\"could not be measured\" is publishable; being SIGKILLed is not). Split into separate criteria per\nthe P6 advisory, each with how it is verified — notably that the GRANT is asserted, not merely\nthat the run completed.\n\n### The admission cap is a real trap\n\nThe rewritten description came out at **8,596 chars against the 8,000 admission cap** — which would\nhave blocked the very re-review the gate demands as the remedy. Trimmed narrative (not evidence:\nevery measurement, line number and timestamp is retained) to **7,995**, applied with no\n`description_warning`. Worth stating plainly for the next person: the remedy for a BLOCK is\nusually to say MORE, and the cap pushes the other way, so budget for it.\n\nFourth run started.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788648039337492335,"uuid":"2f1d3bae-4804-4081-9fcd-32fab3e1a61b"}